# Street Pixels — general rules

## Document authority

- `docs/STREET_PIXELS_PRODUCT_SPEC.md` is the product source of truth. Never
  edit it as part of an implementation task.
- `docs/street-pixels-technical-audit.md` is a dated baseline (audit date
  2026-07-20), not a description of current code. Verify against the working
  tree before relying on it, and report differences instead of assuming the
  audit is current. Never edit it as part of an implementation task.
- `docs/implementation/README.md` is the roadmap and index.
- `docs/implementation/DECISIONS.md` records confirmed decisions and, in §15,
  open questions that are explicitly not decided.
- `docs/implementation/phases/` and `docs/implementation/work-items/` hold the
  detail.

## Scope discipline

- Do not change product scope without explicit instruction. Adding, removing, or
  reinterpreting a requirement is a product decision.
- Keep work limited to the approved work item. If something else needs fixing,
  record it in that work item's discovered-follow-up field.
- Do not modify unrelated code. If a change touches a file the work item does
  not name, say so and explain why.
- Do not proceed into the next work item automatically. Finish, report, stop.
- iOS is not in Android V1 scope. Public Explorer Pro purchasing is not in
  Android V1 scope.
- No city allowlists, pilot-only runtime behaviour, or city-specific feature
  restrictions. The product is worldwide.

## Product invariants

These hold in every build, under every flag, in every code path.

- Raw GPS samples and recorded tracks are never uploaded anywhere.
- Pixel collection happens only during an active, non-paused recording session.
- Imported exploration never affects competition: no recency, no ownership, no
  eligibility, no weekly ranking.
- Offline-first behaviour is preserved. Map viewing, recording, collection,
  progress, track storage, and supported routing work with no network.
- No interpolated exploration across a pause, an interruption, or a rejected
  sample.

## Reporting

- Report contradictions between the spec, the audit, and the code. Do not
  silently pick one.
- Do not weaken, skip, delete, or narrow tests to make an implementation pass.
  If a test blocks the change, report the conflict.
- Do not fabricate or summarise test results. Only executed output counts.
- Do not mark work accepted, and do not mark phases or work items completed.
  Report implementation and validation evidence; a human decides acceptance.
- Cursor checkpoints are not commits or branches. Real Git history is required.

## Repository conventions

Follow `docs/CONTRIBUTING.md` and `docs/PR_GUIDE.md`: subsystem prefixes such as
`[map]` or `[android]`, imperative mood, English, sign-off required
(`git commit -s`), formatting changes separate from logical changes.
